From 9fe35fbfda3253106c77275aa40d4c0b595d6bfb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Michaud <jmichaud@igalia.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 10:52:48 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 03/17] [MYA] Add mya_heap and liblldb + rtti as dependencies.
 https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=325546

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

we add an optional dependency to mya, mya_heap:
- liblldb (installed via homebrew or linux package manager) is used to read debug info
- rtti is added as a compile option to the target process (jsc, WebCore, etc)

This will be useful in the future when we teach mya how to walk the full native heap.

We add a simple test that confirms that we can use both of these from within mya.


Every build change the heap walk needs that is gated on MYA_HEAP, plus the
liblldb changes. They belong here because this is where MYA_HEAP is defined.
Builds without MYA_HEAP are unaffected, except for the DEBUG_FISSION move
(6 below), which changes the order of flags but not the flags themselves.
Production and Release builds never enable MYA_HEAP.

1. Drop RTTI and default symbol visibility again.
   This PR turned on -frtti and turned off -fvisibility=hidden,
   GCC_SYMBOLS_PRIVATE_EXTERN and TAPI, so that mya could name an object's
   dynamic type through its std::type_info. The heap walk names dynamic types
   from DWARF instead: the destructor in an object's vtable, matched against
   the destructors' linkage names, gives its class. Nothing needs a
   type_info, so GCC_ENABLE_CPP_RTTI goes back to NO in every project. JSC,
   WTF and bmalloc hide their symbols again, JSC's TAPI comes back, and
   CMake's -frtti and default-visibility presets go.
   Why: MYA_HEAP Debug builds then compile and link like any other Debug
   build, differing only in debug info. RTTI and export-everything made
   images bigger and links slower, and they hid export-list and ODR mistakes.
   TypeinfoTest stops reading the type_info out of the target (a -fno-rtti
   build emits none) and checks only what liblldb gives: the class's size, its
   base and a member read at the debug info's offset.
   Not changed: the export-all and TAPI settings this PR added to WebCore,
   PAL, WebGPU, WebKit, ANGLE and libwebrtc, and the
   check-for-weak-vtables-and-externals exception. The prototype still had
   them when it was tested. They were added for RTTI and are probably
   removable now, but that has not been verified.

2. Link Xcode's LLDB.framework, not Homebrew's liblldb (Xcode).
   Link with -F$(DEVELOPER_DIR)/../SharedFrameworks and -framework LLDB, with
   rpaths to Xcode's copy and the Command Line Tools' copy. The SB API headers
   still come from Homebrew's llvm, because Xcode doesn't ship them, and the
   SB API is binary compatible across versions.
   Why: Homebrew's liblldb crashes reading WebCore's debug info. Xcode's
   LLDB reads it, and it understands the debug info Xcode's clang writes.

3. Require liblldb 19 or newer (CMake).
   FindLLDB.cmake reports LLDB_VERSION, taken from the library's file name or
   install directory, and JavaScriptCore asks for 19. An older liblldb is a
   configure error that says what to install.
   Why: liblldb 18 can't resolve addresses in an ELF segment that a PT_TLS
   segment starts at, which is where libstdc++'s vtables live. Objects with
   libstdc++ vtables then have no dynamic type. The default liblldb on
   Debian 12 and Ubuntu 24.04 is older than 19, so without the check, builds
   succeed and the heap tests fail with no hint why.

4. Write dSYMs (macOS).
   DEBUG_INFORMATION_FORMAT = dwarf-with-dsym for JavaScriptCore, WebCore,
   WebGPU and WebKit under MYA_HEAP. In CMake, a POST_BUILD dsymutil step
   writes the dSYM next to the image, or next to the framework bundle.
   Why: through the debug map (.o files), liblldb can't find the compile unit
   that defines a global variable, so globals have no type. Also, with
   compilation caching, an object's debug info points at its precompiled
   header's debug info in the cache, which only dsymutil reads. That drops
   the types only the prefix header describes.
   Cost: dsymutil runs after every link.

5. Keep complete linkage and template names (CMake).
   Under MYA_HEAP, take `-mllvm -dwarf-linkage-names=Abstract` and
   -gsimple-template-names back out of the global flags, and don't build
   PCHs with -fpch-debuginfo.
   Why: with Abstract linkage names, an implicit destructor has no linkage
   name, so classes with one get no dynamic type. With simple template names,
   liblldb merges same-sized instances of one class template into a single
   class, so values get another instance's members. With -fpch-debuginfo,
   other compile units only declare the PCH's types, and liblldb resolves a
   declared template instance without its arguments.
   Cost: larger debug info and slower links. These flags exist to shrink
   debug info.

6. Turn off split DWARF (CMake).
   ENABLE_MYA_HEAP forces DEBUG_FISSION off. To do that, the code that
   applies DEBUG_FISSION moves from OptionsCommon.cmake to WebKitCommon.cmake,
   after Options${PORT}, because ENABLE_MYA_HEAP is only known there. The
   option is still declared in OptionsCommon.cmake.
   Why: liblldb indexes .debug_names, not .gdb_index. With split DWARF it
   opens and indexes every .dwo file each time it loads an image.
   Effect on other builds: the same -gsplit-dwarf and --gdb-index flags,
   added later in the flag list.

7. Link with --build-id (non-Apple).
   Why: an image's build-id is the Linux equivalent of LC_UUID. mya checks it
   so that a file rebuilt since the target loaded it is refused instead of
   misread, and it leaves out of the image list any image without one. lld
   writes no build-id unless asked, and only some distributions' clang
   drivers ask. Without this, WebKit's own images are missing from the list
   on Linux, and `mya heap` can't find JavaScriptCore.

8. Export libpas's API (PAS_BMALLOC_HIDDEN=0).
   In Xcode, added to WK_MYA_HEAP_PREPROCESSOR_DEFINITIONS_YES. In CMake,
   added as a compile definition, and the Cocoa bmalloc build stops forcing
   PAS_BMALLOC_HIDDEN=1.
   Why: the heap walk runs libpas's own enumerator (pas_enumerator) over the
   snapshot to account for every allocation. On Linux it also builds the
   enumerator's root with pas_root_create and pas_heap_lock_lock/unlock. The
   HeapWalk tests enumerate their own heap the same way. All of these are
   calls from libJavaScriptCoreTools into libpas, which lives inside
   JavaScriptCore and bmalloc. Without the exports, libJavaScriptCoreTools
   fails to link once the heap walk lands.

9. Run testLibJSCTools with OS_ACTIVITY_MODE=disable on Cocoa.
   Why: Xcode's LLDB.framework (2) logs an os_signpost and an os_log message
   for every SB API call, which doubles the time of the heap walk tests.

The PR title and description should drop "rtti", for example
"[MYA] Add mya_heap: debug info builds and liblldb".
